$DIR/main.p:9:26: cannot return make(chan int) as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the channel, whose reference temp drops when it returns
    The chan int made at main.p:9 is a temporary: nothing keeps a reference to
    it after the statement, and an ordinary interface that lends it counts no
    reference of its own, so the interface may not outlive that statement.
    Converting make(chan int) to an ordinary interface lends it: the interface
    points at the channel without a reference of its own.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the channel itself, changing the result type to chan int: the caller
    gets a reference of its own:
        return make(chan int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:11:37: cannot return c as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the channel, whose reference param drops when it returns
    c (declared at main.p:11) is a chan int, a counted reference to a channel:
    an ordinary interface that lends the channel does not count a reference of
    its own, so it may not outlive c, whose reference is dropped at the end of
    its scope in param. Converting c to an ordinary interface lends it: the
    interface points at the channel without a reference of its own.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the channel itself, changing the result type to chan int: the caller
    gets a reference of its own:
        return c
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:13:26: cannot store c in package variable kept, which outlives c
    c (declared at main.p:13) is a chan int, a counted reference to a channel:
    an ordinary interface that lends the channel does not count a reference of
    its own, so it may not outlive c, whose reference is dropped at the end of
    its scope in store. When store returns, c drops its reference, and the
    channel is freed if that was the last one, but package variables live for
    the whole program, so package variable kept would be left pointing at
    reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store c itself, changing the type of kept to chan int: it then counts a
    reference of its own:
        kept = c
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
